new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6e8377a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/isolated-backup-protocol.md
@@ -0,0 +1,107 @@
+# Isolated runtime backup protocol v1
+
+Status: Agent-side implementation for native fixed-slot runtimes. This is not
+a legacy-agent API, a ClawManager deployment change, or an end-to-end backup
+acceptance result. The zhanghui08 site returned 404 for the legacy
+`/v1/stop-capabilities` route on 2026-10-29. That observation alone does not
+identify its image source revision or establish support for this protocol.
+
+## Scope and caller
+
+ClawManager D's runtime control owner is the intended caller. Before a mutation
+it must validate the original task/attempt authority, namespace and Pod UID,
+raw source inventory, current binding, complete participant barrier, encrypted
+original create request and reserved successor generation. It journals the
+intent before dispatch and performs the final authority check before accepting
+the receipt. B consumes D callbacks; it must not construct control SQL itself.
+
+All routes require the existing `X-ClawManager-Control-Token`. Deployment must
+provide the existing trusted management transport. The token grants Agent
+control access, not a new backup task authority. The API has no arbitrary shell,
+path, SQL, namespace discovery or user-selected process target.
+
+The stop scope is `instance_pid_namespaces_and_mounts`: the isolated lifecycle
+owner joins its tracked auxiliary commands, stops the sandbox process tree and
+cleans execution resources before the receipt is persisted. It does not prove
+that other Pods, installations or external NFS clients cannot write the same
+directory. Installation ownership of the NFS subtree and all other participants
+remain mandatory controller checks. A shared NFS export is never the backup
+scope by itself.
+
+## Wire contract
+
+Shared request/receipt definitions and digest helper are in
+`clawmanager-agent/gateway/backup_protocol.go`. Routes are registered by the
+native `slots.Worker` and `slots.Pool` constructors:
+
+| Route | Input | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| GET `/v1/backup/capabilities?gateway_port=N` | Exact slot primary port | Metadata only; never a stop proof |
+| POST `/v1/backup/stop` | `BackupRequest` | Persisted `BackupReceipt`, state `stopped` |
+| POST `/v1/backup/resume` | `BackupResumeRequest` | 202 while starting, or persisted receipt, state `resumed` |
+| POST `/v1/backup/state` | Original `BackupRequest` | Current operation receipt, or 202 while starting |
+| POST `/v1/backup/complete` | Original `BackupRequest` | Resumed receipt; releases the backup fence after controller CAS |
+
+`BackupRequest` binds protocol `isolated-backup.v1`, namespace, Pod UID,
+per-worker Agent epoch, 32-character lowercase hex operation ID, runtime type,
+instance/user IDs, original generation/gateway ID, exact primary port, expected
+PID, reserved successor generation, and restart digest. The epoch is obtained
+from that slot's new capability endpoint, not the pool's legacy capability API.
+
+`RestartSHA256` is the lowercase SHA-256 of
+`json.Marshal(gateway.CreateGatewayRequest)` as defined in this protocol's
+source revision. Use `gateway.BackupRestartDigest`; do not hash a different
+DTO, ciphertext, YAML or user-supplied JSON bytes. This includes the exact
+Pod UID, security profile, UID/GID, workspace, port block, generation and all
+configuration fields. The pool forwards the request unchanged. Secret values
+are carried only in the authenticated resume request and runtime memory; the
+proof journals contain the digest, not the configuration.
+
+## Durable transitions and retries
+
+1. `stop` persists `stopping` in the shared instance ledger and slot journal
+   before cancellation. The stable instance flock stays held. The manager pins
+   the current generation/PID and cancels its lifecycle under the same mutex.
+2. Only completed cleanup can persist `stopped`. HTTP 404, missing manager
+   state, a capability response or a stopped root PID cannot create a receipt.
+   A repeated identical stop returns the same persisted receipt. A different
+   operation is rejected.
+3. Ordinary creates, deletes, pending cancellations and maintenance mutations
+   are rejected while the backup fence is active. Other slots/Pods cannot claim
+   the durable backup state, even if the original flock disappears in a crash.
+4. `resume` requires the exact authorized payload and positive stop receipt. It
+   persists `resuming` and the successor assignment before CreateGateway. A
+   failed/unknown create is never dispatched again on retry. Retries observe
+   the same reserved generation; absence stays an unknown outcome.
+5. A positive running identity produces the persisted resumed receipt. The
+   controller must commit its original `CompleteRestart` binding CAS before
+   calling `complete`. Until then ordinary mutation remains fenced.
+
+Controller restart with the same live Agent can reconcile by the bound state
+route. Agent process restart is deliberately different: a persisted active
+backup is quarantined and capability metadata reports
+`agent_restart_reconciliation: false`. The old receipt remains evidence but
+cannot establish a current stop barrier. Automatic cross-incarnation recovery
+requires an additional reconciliation protocol; changing epoch, deleting the
+ledger or inventing a fresh operation is not a supported recovery procedure.
+Persistence errors also quarantine because a rename/fsync may have an unknown
+commit outcome.
+
+## Validation and outstanding integration
+
+On 2026-10-29, the Linux `slots` and `gateway` test binaries passed in local
+Docker Desktop WSL. The new cases use a simulated process starter and real
+temporary-file persistence/flocks. They cover current-PID drift, cleanup
+failure, operation replacement, ordinary/cross-slot writer exclusion, secret
+absence from journals, configuration digest mismatch, dispatch retry, resume
+completion, process restart quarantine and persistence failure. Existing
+environment-gated kernel/container acceptance cases remain gated; this run is
+not evidence of real sandbox, MySQL, Kubernetes or shared-NFS operation.
+
+Still required in ClawManager D: a new client/capability projection, native
+original-request DTO/custody compatibility, operation-bound SQL receipt storage,
+real gateway callbacks replacing the DELETE/404 flow, source binding CAS using
+the new PID, deployment wiring, complete participants and scoped NFS admission.
+The legacy site cannot be upgraded by replacing an image tag alone. Its
+historical original-generation configurations and existing database import
+profile are separate prerequisites.
